, e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nBlackJoker: 1, 2: 1, A: 4, K: 3, Q: 2, J: 3, 10: 1, 9: 1, 7: 1, 6: 1, 5: 1, 4: 1, 3: 3\nValid Moves:\nsingle: ♣K\nsingle: ♦2\nsingle: RedJoker\npass: []\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation:\n\nLet me analyze the situation:\n\n**Current State:**\n- Landlord (opponent) has only 6 cards left - very dangerous!\n- My teammate has 17 cards - needs significant help\n- I have 10 cards including strong ones (RedJoker, ♦2, ♣K)\n- Landlord just played ♦Q (a single card)\n\n**Valid options:**\n1. **♣K** - beats ♦Q with King\n2. **♦2** - beats ♦Q with a 2 (very strong)\n3. **RedJoker** - beats ♦Q with the strongest single card\n4. **Pass** - let it go to my teammate\n\n**Strategic Analysis:**\n\nOption 1 (♣K): Reasonable mid-strength play. Saves my power cards (2 and RedJoker) for later. However, the landlord only has 6 cards and might have stronger singles.\n\nOption 2 (♦2): Very strong play but uses up one of my most powerful cards. The landlord has only 1 remaining 2 (from card counting), so this could force them to use their last 2 or a joker.\n\nOption 3 (RedJoker): Wastes my strongest card on a Queen. This is overkill and strategically poor.\n\nOption 4 (Pass): My teammate has 17 cards and needs to start playing. However, passing gives control to my teammate who may not have cards to beat Q, giving the landlord another free turn when they\'re at 6 cards.\n\n**Critical consideration:** The landlord is at 6 cards - extremely dangerous. I must not let them gain momentum. Passing risks giving them control back if my teammate can\'t beat it or also passes.\n\n**Best Move:** Play ♦2. This applies maximum pressure on the landlord with their remaining cards, potentially forcing out their last 2 or BlackJoker. I still retain RedJoker as my ultimate weapon.\n\nReason: With the landlord at only 6 cards, I cannot afford to be passive. Playing ♦2 puts extreme pressure on them and forces them to use high-value cards or pass, giving me control. This aggressive approach is necessary when the opponent is close to winning. I keep RedJoker for absolute emergencies.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♦2"]}'}
